  },
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Bonding in which of the following diatomic molecule(s) become(s) stronger, on the basis of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>MO</mi></math> Theory, by removal of an electron?</p><p>(A) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NO</mi></math></p><p>(B) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p><p>(C) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p><p>(D) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p><p>(E)&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p><p>Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :</p>"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;only",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;only",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;only",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;only",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">In all these molecules&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#8203;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>and</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>NO</mi></math>, is stabilized by removal of electrons because it is having electrons in the anti-bonding orbital. And thus removal of electron from the anti bonding orbital, increases the bond order of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>and</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>NO</mi></math>​. And thus is becomes more stable after removal of electrons. Bond order of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>&#8203;</mo></math>is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math>&nbsp;and it is more&nbsp;than that of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>​&nbsp;which is having bond order as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>. Similarly Bond order of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NO</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>is</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>and</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>that</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>NO</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>is</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>3</mn></math>.</p>"
  }
}